WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.740
Good Manufacturing Practice requires manufacturing
operations to follow approved instructions and

2
00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:08.070
appropriate controls. Processes should be

3
00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:11.200
defined, suitable for
their intended purpose,

4
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.670
and performed by trained
personnel. Controls

5
00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:16.780
must address potential
contamination, mix-ups

6
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.910
and errors throughout the process. This

7
00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:21.820
introductory lesson
explains common operating

8
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.670
principles. Specific
requirements depend on

9
00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:28.010
the product, dosage form
and manufacturing process.

10
00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:34.210
Incoming starting materials and packaging
components should be received, identified, and

11
00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:39.630
controlled according to approved procedures.
Appropriate quarantine and release arrangements help

12
00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.230
prevent unintended use. Storage conditions

13
00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:45.130
and stock rotation should reflect material

14
00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:48.410
requirements. Before
dispensing, personnel should

15
00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:51.990
verify material identity,
status and suitability.

16
00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:57.060
A label alone may not be sufficient when
supporting records indicate a discrepancy.

17
00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:02.120
Dispensing involves selecting and measuring
the correct approved materials for a

18
00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.850
defined batch. Operators
should follow authorized

19
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:08.500
instructions, use suitable
calibrated instruments,

20
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.710
and document quantities
and material identities.

21
00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.000
Appropriate independent
checks or technical

22
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.120
controls may be required by site
procedures and risk. Traceability connects

23
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.410
each dispensed material to its source
and the batch in which it is used.

24
00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:28.670
Before manufacturing begins, line
clearance and setup checks help ensure that

25
00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:33.830
previous materials, labels, documents
and residues do not create mix-up or

26
00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:38.870
contamination risks. The equipment, area
and materials should be confirmed suitable

27
00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:43.630
for the planned operation. Checks
must follow approved procedures and be

28
00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:48.790
recorded as required. The exact sequence
and verification method depend on

29
00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:50.150
the facility and process.

30
00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:55.230
In-process controls provide information
about the state of a manufacturing process

31
00:01:55.230 --> 00:02:01.470
while it is running. Examples may include
weight, volume, temperature, pressure,

32
00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:03.870
pH, appearance or other process-specific

33
00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:07.250
measurements. Monitoring
frequency, limits and

34
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:11.590
response actions must come from the
approved process and supporting scientific

35
00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:17.390
justification. There is no single universal
acceptance criterion that applies to

36
00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:18.930
every pharmaceutical process.

37
00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:22.290
Batch manufacturing
records provide traceable

38
00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.490
evidence of the actual
operations performed.

39
00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:27.930
Entries should identify
relevant materials,

40
00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:32.110
equipment, operators, times,
process observations and

41
00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:36.950
checks as required by the approved
record. Reconciliation can help detect

42
00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:39.650
unexplained differences
in quantities or printed

43
00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.590
materials. Documentation must reflect what

44
00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.790
occurred, including
deviations, and must not

45
00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:48.000
be completed retrospectively
from guesswork.

46
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:51.490
Unexpected process
results, equipment alarms

47
00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:53.640
or procedural departures should be handled

48
00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:57.780
under the site quality system.
Operators should secure the process as

49
00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.450
appropriate, report the event and preserve

50
00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:03.540
relevant data. Authorized personnel assess

51
00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.050
product impact and
determine investigation,

52
00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:09.840
disposition and corrective
actions. A result

53
00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.920
that later returns to normal does
not automatically eliminate the need

54
00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.710
to document and evaluate
the original event.

55
00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:21.370
Imagine an operator preparing to
start a batch who discovers that

56
00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:25.330
a container of starting material
has a released label but the

57
00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:30.930
electronic inventory record still shows
quarantine status. The operator should not

58
00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:34.990
choose whichever status seems convenient.
The material should be held from

59
00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:38.570
use while authorized
personnel reconcile the

60
00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:41.650
discrepancy under
approved procedures. This

61
00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:45.650
illustrates why status
verification, traceability

62
00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:48.280
and escalation matter
before manufacturing begins.
